---
// The money page (WEBSITE.md §5.5), rewritten as the pay-what-you-want page
// (#1876, maintainer decisions by poll 2026-10-23).
//
// WHAT CHANGED, AND WHAT DID NOT. Until 2026-10-23 this page said "OurHike
// takes no donations today, and it will never take one on a trail
// organization's behalf". The second half still holds: the direct-giving
// links at the foot of the page are unchanged, and nothing a hiker gives a
// club passes through OurHike. The first half does not: OurHike now asks
// hikers to cover its own costs, pay-what-you-want with $0 on the list. That
// supersedes in part features/PRICING_MODEL.md's pricing value #6 ("money
// never passes through OurHike"), whose 2026-08-27 banner is amended in the
// same change rather than left to contradict this page.
//
// THE STORY IS ONE MAN'S RECOLLECTION, AND THE PAGE SAYS SO. The quote is
// Dave Field's, read 2026-10-23 on the episode page of The Green Tunnel's
// "Changemakers" (2021-11-17). No independent source for the resignation was
// found, and one record sits awkwardly beside it: the AT Museum lists Marion
// Park as the Maine Appalachian Trail Club's treasurer from 1937 to 1957, so
// any term of Stephenson's came later and its year is unknown. That is why
// the page quotes Field and attributes it, rather than stating it as history
// in its own voice. Stephenson's ATC roles are from appalachiantrailhistory.org
// (read the same day). And the story is about profit, not price: the guides
// were sold, and the line she held was against a premium on top of cost. The
// page must not retell it as "maps were given away".
//
// THE COMMITMENTS ARE THE FOUNDER'S, DATED. Every line in "Where the money
// goes" and "How it is held" is the maintainer's own decision, 2026-10-23.
// The fee figures are Buy Me a Coffee's published rates (help article "How
// to calculate charges on your payment", read 2026-10-23: 5% platform, Stripe
// 2.9% + 30c, 0.5% subscription, 0.5% payout) with the arithmetic done here;
// international cards pay 1% more and the table does not show it.
//
// THE RUNNING TOTAL IS LIVE OR IT IS ABSENT. The maintainer chose to publish
// the year's total rather than cut anything off at the $100,000 line. It is
// read from the backend's public /support/total when the build was given
// PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL and that answers; otherwise the page says the total is
// not available. It never prints a figure it did not just read.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const title = 'Pay what you want — OurHike'
const description =
  'OurHike is free for every hiker, and pay-what-you-want with $0 on the list. Why maps should not be sold for profit, how choosing works, and where every dollar goes.'

const apiBase = (import.meta.env.PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL ?? '').replace(/\/+$/, '')

const history =
  'https://appalachiantrailhistory.org/exhibits/show/builders/jeanstephenson'

const episode =
  'https://podcasts.rrchnm.org/show/the-green-tunnel/changemakers/'

// Per yearly payment: 5% platform + 2.9% + 30c card + 0.5% subscription +
// 0.5% payout. Rounded to the cent, which is what a hiker sees.
const fees = [
  { tier: '$1', fee: '$0.39', kept: '$0.61' },
  { tier: '$10', fee: '$1.19', kept: '$8.81' },
  { tier: '$100', fee: '$9.20', kept: '$90.80' },
]
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="wrap">
    <header class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Trails are free. So is this map.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        OurHike is pay-what-you-want, and <strong>$0 is one of the
        answers</strong>. The map, water, closures and hazards are free for
        every hiker, forever. Once a year, on your fifth day out with OurHike,
        we ask you to choose what you give: $0, $1, $10 or $100 a year. Every
        answer keeps everything working.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section--tight prose">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>A guide is a service to the hiker</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="para">
        Jean Stephenson was
        <a href={history}>“essentially ATC Chairman Myron Avery's second in
        command”</a> at the Appalachian Trail Conference, and the author or
        editor of guidebooks hikers carried for decades. Those guides were
        sold. What they were not was a source of profit, and Dave Field, who
        worked alongside her and is still a member of the Maine Appalachian
        Trail Club, remembers the day that line was tested:
      </p>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            Jean was following the policy that she and Myron Avery had set was
            that the trail guides were services to the hiker not to be used for
            profit. Well, Steve suggested a small premium be charged to help pay
            for the next edition of the guide. And before the argument was over,
            Jean burst into tears and resigned on the spot as treasurer of the
            Maine Appalachian Trail Club.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>
          Dave Field, in
          <a href={episode}>“Changemakers,” <cite>The Green Tunnel</cite></a>
          (R2 Studios, George Mason University), November 17, 2021. His
          recollection; we have not found the year it happened.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="para">
        She would not take a premium even to pay for the next edition. OurHike
        holds the same line and goes one step further: the map costs nothing at
        all. What pays for the next edition is what hikers choose to give, and
        nobody pays a premium they did not choose.
      </p>
      <p class="para">
        That matters because a trail map is safety equipment. A hiker who
        cannot afford a guide still walks the trail; the only question is
        whether they know where the water is. Trails are open to everyone,
        whatever they earn, and so is this map. That is why $0 is on the list,
        and why nothing safety-relevant will ever sit behind a payment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>How choosing works</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li>
          <strong>Day five.</strong> On your fifth day using OurHike in a
          calendar year, the app asks once. The days are counted on your phone
          and never sent anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick $0, $1, $10 or $100 a year.</strong> $0 needs nothing:
          no account, no reason, no follow-up. It asks again next year.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A paid choice is set up on the web, never in the app.</strong>
          It goes through Buy Me a Coffee, so no app store takes a cut. You
          sign in to OurHike so the app knows on every device you use. In the
          iPhone and Android apps the link comes by email, because those stores
          do not allow it in the app.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stopping is one tap.</strong> More → Your support → Stop. We
          cancel it for you the same day; you never have to find your way
          through somebody else's account page.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="fees prose">
        <p class="para">
          Card fees come out of every payment. On a yearly payment, this is
          what reaches OurHike:
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>You give</th><th>Fees</th><th>Reaches OurHike</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              fees.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{row.tier}</td>
                  <td>{row.fee}</td>
                  <td>{row.kept}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note">
          Buy Me a Coffee's published rates: 5% to the platform, 2.9% + 30¢ to
          the card processor, and 0.5% each on subscription payments and
          payouts. Cards issued outside the US pay 1% more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Where the money goes</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards" style="max-width: 980px">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Running costs first.</h3>
          <p>Hosting, storage, the domain, the developer accounts the app stores charge for.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Then growth.</h3>
          <p>Anything beyond running costs goes to marketing and promotion, so more hikers and more trail clubs find OurHike.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>None of it is the founder's income.</h3>
          <p>Not a salary, not a dollar. That is the founder's commitment, and the yearly accounting is how you can check it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>At most 3% to pay developers.</h3>
          <p>If OurHike ever grows to the size of a venture-backed app and has to pay developers, no more than 3% of what comes in goes to that.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Then, the clubs.</h3>
          <p>The long-term aim is money flowing from OurHike to the clubs that maintain the trail. That starts once OurHike is a nonprofit that can make those grants.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>How it is held, now and next</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="stages">
        <li>
          <span class="stage">Now</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Buy Me a Coffee, under the founder's own tax status.</strong>
            The founder pays any tax on it personally. Support is not
            tax-deductible, because OurHike is not a nonprofit yet. This stage
            is limited to $100,000 a year, and the running total is published
            below so anyone can hold us to it.
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="stage">Next</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Open Source Collective as fiscal host.</strong> Every
            dollar in and out goes on a public ledger. The host keeps 10%, and
            support is still not tax-deductible there.
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="stage">Later</span>
          <div>
            <strong>A nonprofit owns OurHike</strong>, so that the hiking
            clubs who maintain the trail benefit the most from it.
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="total" data-api={apiBase}>
        <p class="total__label">This year so far</p>
        <p class="total__figure" data-total-figure>Not available</p>
        <p class="total__note" data-total-note>
          Support has not been switched on yet. Once it is, the year's running
          total appears here, read live from our server.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Give to the trail directly</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards" style="max-width: 980px">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The trail needs money today, too.</h3>
          <p>
            The
            <a href="https://appalachiantrail.org/">Appalachian Trail
            Conservancy</a>, clubs like the
            <a href="https://www.nynjtc.org/">New York–New Jersey Trail
            Conference</a>, and preserves like
            <a href="https://www.mohonkpreserve.org/">Mohonk Preserve</a>
            maintain the ground this app describes, and take donations on
            their own sites. NY State Parks and NYS DEC maintain trails here
            too, funded through the state rather than a donation page.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>We take no cut of that.</h3>
          <p>
            What you give them goes to them, on their own sites, and never
            passes through OurHike. We are not affiliated with any of them; we
            just think your money belongs with the people holding the tools.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Other ways to help</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Report what you find on the trail from inside the app — a dry spring, a
        blowdown, a washed-out crossing reaches a moderator who can act on it.
        Or help build the thing itself:
        <a href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike">the whole project is open
        source</a>, and the issue tracker is where the work is.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  // The published total, read live or not at all. Formats whole dollars;
  // the endpoint answers in cents so nothing here rounds money twice.
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.total')
  const api = box?.dataset.api
  if (box && api) {
    fetch(`${api}/support/total`)
      .then((r) => (r.ok ? r.json() : Promise.reject(r.status)))
      .then((body: { year: number; total_cents: number; supporters: number }) => {
        const figure = box.querySelector('[data-total-figure]')
        const note = box.querySelector('[data-total-note]')
        if (!figure || !note) return
        const dollars = Math.floor(body.total_cents / 100).toLocaleString('en-US')
        figure.textContent = `$${dollars}`
        note.textContent = `Given in ${body.year} by ${body.supporters.toLocaleString('en-US')} ${body.supporters === 1 ? 'supporter' : 'supporters'}, before fees. The $100,000 line is this stage's limit.`
      })
      .catch(() => {
        // Leave "Not available" standing: an honest unknown, never a guess.
      })
  }
</script>

<style>
  .para {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--fg-1);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .quote {
    margin: 22px 0 24px;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 22px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blaze-orange);
  }

  .quote blockquote p {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .quote figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .steps strong,
  .stages strong {
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .fees {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }

  .fees table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 460px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .fees th,
  .fees td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 8px 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-1);
  }

  .fees th {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .stages {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .stages li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 64px 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .stage {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--brand-primary);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    padding-top: 2px;
  }

  .total {
    margin-top: 28px;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-1);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 20px 24px;
  }

  .total__label {
    font-size: 12.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .total__figure {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 32px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--fg-1);
    margin: 4px 0 6px;
  }

  .total__note {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
</style>
